  "body": "What happens:\nCamCraft has Rotary Contour (waterline rings), Rotary Parallel (generatrix passes along axis), and Rotary Offset (radial roughing) for turned parts. It also has Flow5D (auto-computed direction field, 5-axis tilt) and MultiAxisContour (picked edge chains, 5-axis tilt). There is no operation that combines:\n- A rotary axis (A/B/C) as primary continuous motion\n- Picked edge chains defining helical flow lines\n- Radial tool pose (tool axis points to rotation axis)\n- Laid-down planning frame (like Rotary Parallel)\n\nExpected:\nA new \"Rotary Flow\" operation (also called Cylindrical Flow) that:\n1. Fits the axis of revolution from the model (shared with rotary twins)\n2. Lays the part down in a planning frame (local X = axis, local Z ≈ world up)\n3. Accepts picked edge chains that define helical flow lines on the surface\n4. Generates passes along local X that follow the picked chains in the laid frame\n5. Steps between chains in local Y (which maps to rotary angle)\n6. Poses the tool radially on every cut move (shared radial pose law)\n7. Stamps C-axis angles when the machine has a spindle C (shared with Rotary Contour)\n\nEvidence:\nRequested for machining injection screws, augers, propeller blades, turbine blades - any helical surface where the user defines flow lines by picking edges.\n\nDone means:\n- New operation kind \"Rotary Flow\" in MultiAxis section\n- Requires ball-nose tool\n- Accepts edge chain picks as geometry source\n- Parameters: stepover (distance between flow lines), tolerance, clearance/retract/top/bottom Z, feed, plunge_feed, climb, lead/lag/max_tilt_deg\n- Works on mill-turn (C-axis) and 4-axis mill (A/B-axis)\n- Posts C-axis angles when machine has spindle C\n- Verified by stock simulation and fixture checks",
